1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Jeet started with one question: the jeet sold, but is he really out?

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This sprint turned that question into a field tool.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
So I built Jeet to reconstruct
the wallet's actual token lifecycle.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Here is a real Solana case
I anonymized for the demo.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Wallet A sold about 7.52 million tokens.
Its balance actually hit zero.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
At that point, Jeet could defensibly
classify the wallet as VERIFIED OUT.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
But that was not the end of the story.

8
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Jeet continued following the lifecycle and found
Wallet A later bought back almost 5.97 million tokens.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The historical answer was true:
the wallet was out.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The current answer is different:
RE-ENTERED. NOT OUT.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
That distinction is the point of Jeet Analyzer.

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:07,000
The Telegram bot reuses Jeet's jobs, budgets, access ledger, and protected reports.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,000
It does not create a second forensic engine or guess at ownership.

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
In this case, common control is NOT PROVEN.

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Downstream relationship coverage
is explicitly marked incomplete.

16
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Real investigations exposed two generic classification and accounting defects.
Both were fixed and converted into regression tests.

17
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The public build passes 440 backend and 44 frontend tests.

18
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Telegram and payment paths passed the mocked A–Q rehearsal. Production stays OFF, and this replay uses zero provider calls.

19
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
He sold. He was out. Then he came back.

20
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Jeet caught the whole story.
